<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Otfrid of Weissenburg</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 800–after 870</span> · <a href="/centuries/9th-century">9th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Otfrid of Weissenburg was a Benedictine monk of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wissembourg-abbey">Wissembourg Abbey</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alsace">Alsace</a> and the first German poet known by name. Around 863 to 871 he completed his Gospel Harmony, or Evangelienbuch, a retelling of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gospels">Gospels</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/old-high-german">Old High German</a> verse that was the first major German work to use end rhyme instead of alliteration.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/otfrid-of-weissenburg.jpg" alt="Otfrid of Weissenburg" width="320" height="512" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Peter Christian (Pfold) · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Otfriedvonweissenburg.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
